\section{Resolve Names to Identities, Not File Paths}

Consider two libraries that both export a type named \texttt{Frame}.
One is a video library and the other is a layout library. A source
file can import the former as \texttt{Video} and the latter as
\texttt{Layout}; \texttt{Video.Frame} and \texttt{Layout.Frame} denote
different types despite the same final segment. An alias makes a new
binding in an environment. It does not create a second definition
or change which package instance owns the item. Moving a definition
between source files is also distinct from renaming its module:
source discovery and name resolution are different compiler jobs.

Rust's book illustrates paths anchored at a crate root and paths
relative to the current module. OCaml's \texttt{open} illustrates a
different issue: importing members into an unqualified environment
can mask an existing binding. Neither choice requires a resolver to
guess which file happens to be present. In the resolver below, each
package has already provided its root and a table of direct
dependency aliases. The import syntax names an anchor explicitly
when a particular root is intended.

\section{Keep the Namespace and Lookup Tier Explicit}

Our miniature language separates module, type, and value names.
Each intermediate segment of a dotted path is looked up in the
module namespace; its final segment is looked up in the namespace
required by the syntax. It is legal for the type \texttt{Frame} and
its constructor function \texttt{Frame} to coexist. That does not
allow a variable named \texttt{Video} to hijack the module path
\texttt{Video.Frame}. A language with first-class module values
may make another choice, but it still needs a rule for those
intermediate segments.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Use & Starting lookup & Final namespace \\
\hline
\texttt{Frame} in a type & lexical scope tiers & type \\
\texttt{Video.Frame(...)} & local module alias & value \\
\texttt{root.ui.Frame} & current package root & context dependent \\
\texttt{dep.layout.Frame} & declared dependency alias & context dependent \\
\texttt{super.Frame} & enclosing module & context dependent \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Within one scope, our policy gives declarations and explicit imports
precedence over wildcard imports. Two explicit imports that bind
the same name to distinct identities in one namespace are errors at
the declaration sites. Two wildcard matches from that namespace
are an error at the use instead, unless both denote the same
identity. An inner scope shadows an outer scope as a whole: we
test both tiers of the inner scope before checking the outer scope.
This is a particular policy, not a property of importing.
\Cref{fig:visual-398} shows why the alias is not the definition.

