% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Walk a Qualified Path Without Falling Back}

\begin{verbatim}
resolve(path, expected_kind, scope, requester):
    (head, tail) := split_anchor(path)
    if head.is_anchor:
        start := resolve_anchor(head, requester)
    else if tail.is_empty:
        return lexical(head, expected_kind, scope)
    else:
        start := lexical(head, Module, scope).definition
    require_access(start, requester)
    require_nonempty(tail)
    cur := start
    for (segment, is_last) in mark_last(tail):
        kind := expected_kind if is_last else Module
        entry := members_visible_to(cur, requester)[kind, segment]
        if entry is missing:
            fail_at(segment, "not a visible member of", cur)
        cur := entry.definition
    return cur

lexical(name, kind, scope):
    for frame in enclosing_scopes(scope):
        direct := frame.direct[kind, name]
        if direct exists: return direct
        matches := canonicalize(frame.wildcard_matches(kind, name))
        if size(matches) > 1: report_ambiguity(matches)
        if size(matches) == 1: return only(matches)
    report_unbound(name, kind)
\end{verbatim}

In this notation the dependency name is part of the anchor:
\texttt{dep.layout.Frame} uses the package bound to \texttt{layout}
and then walks \texttt{Frame}. Member lookup asks what the
requester may see, including valid public aliases. It does not run
another lexical search at each dot. If an inner \texttt{Video}
shadows an outer one but has no \texttt{Frame}, report the failed
member lookup in the inner one. Falling back to the outer
\texttt{Video} would mean the first segment can change its identity
depending on the remainder of the path.

\section{Preserve Enough Context for Diagnostics and Incrementality}

After resolution, attach a definition identity to each use but
retain the span and import chain. If wildcard imports from distinct
modules produce two candidates, a good diagnostic points to the
use, both imports, and both declarations. It can suggest qualified
alternatives without selecting a candidate arbitrarily. If a user
references a module by a package alias that is not declared, suggest
checking the manifest instead of speculatively searching adjacent
directories for a matching name.

A resolution cache cannot depend only on the declaration it chose.
Today a wildcard scope may contain exactly one \texttt{Frame};
tomorrow a different wildcard source may export another. The
original definition has not changed but the lookup is ambiguous.
Record the searched namespace entries, including misses and the
wildcard export surfaces consulted. As a regression test, insert
the new export and verify that only affected users become
ambiguous. Then make it a re-export of the original and verify the
ambiguity clears while provenance still shows both routes. Repeat
the test with a value namespace addition to ensure it does not
invalidate a type lookup unnecessarily.

\section{Order Resolution Queries Without Consulting the Filesystem}

Import declarations themselves can refer to other aliases. To
keep the example deterministic, suppose such references are
allowed only for explicit imports in the same module. Enter
local declarations and explicit import names into the scope first,
with unresolved import targets marked as pending. Import right
hand sides can query declarations and those pending names but not
the wildcard environment being built. Once explicit imports are
bound, process wildcard sources, then resolve ordinary type and
value expressions. This rule makes the right hand sides independent
of the order in which wildcard candidates happen to arrive.

\begin{verbatim}
import View as Front     // module alias, forward reference
import dep.layout as View
import dep.video as Film
import Front.*           // opens the layout package
import Film.*            // opens the video package

type Output = Frame     // ambiguous between distinct type IDs
type Safe = Front.Frame
\end{verbatim}

The first import triggers the pending lookup of the second; its
resolved answer is installed under both \texttt{Front} and
\texttt{View}. If the second right hand side were \texttt{Front}
instead, the search would revisit an in-progress query. Report the
two alias declarations as a cycle. Do not mistake such a cycle
for two modules that refer to each other's already-declared types.
A resolver can memoize state and outcomes of explicit import
queries and share the referenced definition once; textually
substituting paths multiplies work and makes source spans harder
to preserve.

Now build the example twice with opposite source enumeration
orders. Both runs must choose the same root for \texttt{Front},
reject the same use of \texttt{Frame}, and report candidates in
a stable order. In an editor, inserting a typo in the dependency
alias should strand only lookups affected by that alias, not turn
every error into an unqualified-name fallback. These properties
follow from the resolution environment and the phase boundary,
not from a fortunate ordering of filesystem entries.
